    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kalaniopuu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kalaniopuu.jpg" alt="Kalaniʻōpuʻu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kalaniopuu">Kalaniʻōpuʻu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kalani%CA%BB%C5%8Dpu%CA%BBu_%CA%BBAhu_%CA%BBula_and_mahiole.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mark Miller, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the late eighteenth century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaiian Islands</a> were divided among rival paramount chiefs, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahekili-ii">Kahekili II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a> had extended his rule over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oʻahu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molokai">Molokaʻi</a>. Kamehameha, a chief of the island of Hawaiʻi whom his uncle, the ruling chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalaniopuu">Kalaniʻōpuʻu</a>, had made guardian of the war god Kūkāʻilimoku, fell out with Kalaniʻōpuʻu’s heir after the old ruler’s death in 1782 and defeated him, his cousin Kīwalaʻō, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mokuohai">Battle of Mokuʻōhai</a>, winning the west and north of the island. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">Muskets</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">cannon</a> bought from foreign traders, and two British sailors detained in 1790, John Young and Isaac Davis, who became his advisers, strengthened his forces. He invaded Maui in 1790 and won the Battle of Kepaniwai, and in 1791 his last rival on Hawaiʻi, Keōua Kūʻahuʻula, was killed at the newly built temple of Puʻukoholā. After Kahekili’s death in 1794 Kamehameha struck at his heirs: in 1795 he took Maui and Molokaʻi, landed on Oʻahu and won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nuuanu">Battle of Nuʻuanu</a>, after which Kahekili’s son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalanikupule">Kalanikūpule</a> was captured and sacrificed to the war god. A storm in 1796 and an epidemic in 1804 wrecked his plans to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kauai">Kauaʻi</a>, but in 1810 its ruler, Kaumualiʻi, accepted him as overlord, completing the unification of the islands under one king.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i.jpg" alt="Kamehameha I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_Choris_(1795-1828)_-_Kamehameha,_King_of_the_Sandwich_Islands_by_Louis_Choris,_(Russian),_pen_and_watercolor.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Louis Choris, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hawaiian society rested on a hierarchy of chiefs (<em>aliʻi</em>), priests and commoners (<em>makaʻāinana</em>), bound by the religious restrictions of the <em>kapu</em> system and organised in land divisions, <em>ahupuaʻa</em>, that ran from the uplands to the sea, where irrigated taro, fishponds and fishing fed a dense population. Contact with the wider world transformed this economy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a> kept the export of sandalwood to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> as a royal monopoly; after his death the chiefs bought ships and goods on credit, a tax payable in sandalwood was imposed in 1826 to meet their debts, and by the 1830s the trade had collapsed. From 1819 American whaling ships called for provisions, and in the peak years of the 1840s and 1850s hundreds a year put in at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahaina">Lahaina</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">Sugar</a> grew from the plantation founded at Kōloa on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kauai">Kauaʻi</a> in 1835 into the dominant industry, worked by contract labourers from China from 1852, from Portugal’s Atlantic islands from 1878 and, in large numbers, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> from 1885. Introduced diseases devastated the Hawaiian people: a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 1832 counted about 130,000 inhabitants, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> epidemic in 1853 killed thousands, and in 1890 only some 40,600 of the kingdom’s 89,990 people were Native Hawaiian. Mission schools nonetheless spread literacy in Hawaiian with remarkable speed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nuuanu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-nuuanu.jpg" alt="Battle of Nuʻuanu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nuuanu">Battle of Nuʻuanu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nuuanu_Pali_(1258).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jiang, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a> built his kingdom by war. His warriors fought with spears, clubs and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sling">slings</a>, but he was quick to acquire <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">cannon</a> and Western vessels, and foreigners such as Isaac Davis, sole survivor of the schooner <em>Fair American</em>, seized in 1790, and John Young served him as gunners and trainers. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nuuanu">Battle of Nuʻuanu</a> in 1795 his army, estimated at 10,000 men or more, drove the defenders up the valley, many of them to their deaths over the Pali cliffs. To invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kauai">Kauaʻi</a> he built a fleet of <em>peleleu</em> war canoes, some 800 according to the historian Samuel Kamakau, only for an epidemic to break the expedition in 1804. Later, government troops defeated the defenders of the old religion at Kuamoʻo in December 1819 and crushed a rising on Kauaʻi in 1824. Thereafter the kingdom relied on diplomacy, for it could not resist foreign warships: in 1843 Lord George Paulet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> occupied the islands for five months, and in 1849 French troops seized the fort at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a>. A small army and royal guard remained, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalakaua">Kalākaua</a>’s only warship, the <em>Kaimiloa</em>, sailed to Samoa in 1887, but the Honolulu Rifles, militia of the Hawaiian League, imposed the constitution of 1887, and in January 1893 Queen Liliʻuokalani yielded rather than fight the 162 American sailors and marines landed from USS <em>Boston</em>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kamehameha-iii.jpg" alt="Kamehameha III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-iii">Kamehameha III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Photo_of_Kamehameha_III_(PP-97-7-003).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hugo Stangenwald, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a> ruled as supreme chief, governing the islands through trusted chiefs and appointed governors, controlling the sandalwood trade and upholding the <em>kapu</em> system. His Law of the Splintered Paddle, the Kānāwai Māmalahoe, which protected the weak and travellers on the road, is now written into the constitution of the State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>. From 1819 a <em>kuhina nui</em>, an office combining the roles of regent and chief minister first held by Kamehameha’s widow Kaʻahumanu, shared power with the king. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-iii">Kamehameha III</a> (r. 1825–1854), advised by former missionaries such as William Richards and Gerrit P. Judd, the kingdom became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a>: a declaration of rights in 1839 was followed by the constitution of 1840, which created a House of Nobles, an elected House of Representatives and a supreme court. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-mahele">Great Māhele</a> of 1848 divided the land among the king, the government and the chiefs, and laws of 1850 let commoners claim the plots they farmed and foreigners buy land. A more democratic constitution of 1852 was replaced in 1864 by Kamehameha V, who abolished the office of <em>kuhina nui</em> and strengthened the crown. After the Kamehameha line died out, Lunalilo was elected king in 1873 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalakaua">Kalākaua</a>, by the legislature, in 1874. Ministries, courts and a civil service, with many foreign-born officials, carried out the work of government.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/iolani-palace"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iolani-palace.jpg" alt="ʻIolani Palace" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/iolani-palace">ʻIolani Palace</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iolani_Palace_(51872681413).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gage Skidmore from Surprise, AZ, United States of America, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> showed that a Pacific people could build a state recognised by the great powers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> formally acknowledged its independence in their joint declaration of 28 November 1843, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> confirmed it by treaty in 1849, and the kingdom kept legations and consuls abroad. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalakaua">Kalākaua</a>, often described as the first reigning monarch to travel around the world, made the journey in 1881, and his ministry pursued a Polynesian confederation, signing a treaty with the Samoan ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malietoa-laupepa">Malietoa Laupepa</a> in 1887. At home a literate public sustained a flourishing Hawaiian-language press, beginning with <em>Ka Lama Hawaii</em> in 1834, and Kalākaua’s revival of hula and chant, celebrated at his coronation in 1883, laid foundations for modern Hawaiian culture. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iolani-palace">ʻIolani Palace</a>, completed in 1882 and lit by electricity by 1887, remains the only official royal residence in the United States. Institutions founded by the royal family endure, among them the Queen’s Hospital of 1859 and the Kamehameha Schools, endowed by the will of Princess Bernice Pauahi Bishop. The kingdom’s flag is still the flag of the State of Hawaii, and the memory of the overthrow sustains the Hawaiian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> movement; in 1993 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-congress">United States Congress</a> formally apologised for the American part in it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kalakaua"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kalakaua.jpg" alt="Kalākaua" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kalakaua">Kalākaua</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdavidkalakaua_dust.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">James J. Williams, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom fell through demographic collapse and economic dependence. As the Native Hawaiian population shrank and land passed into foreign hands after the Māhele, American-descended planters and merchants came to dominate an economy that, after the Reciprocity Treaty of 1875, rested on duty-free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> sales to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>; the treaty’s renewal in 1887 gave the United States exclusive use of Pearl Harbor. In mid-1887 the Hawaiian League, backed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a> Rifles, forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalakaua">Kalākaua</a> to dismiss his cabinet and sign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonet-constitution">Bayonet Constitution</a>, which bound the king to his ministers, restricted the vote for the House of Nobles to men with $3,000 in property or $600 in income and denied the vote to Asian residents. A rising led by Robert Wilcox in 1889 failed, the McKinley Tariff of 1890 ended the advantage of Hawaiian sugar, and Kalākaua died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> in January 1891. When his sister, Queen Liliʻuokalani, moved to proclaim a new constitution in January 1893, a Committee of Safety, supported by the American minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-l-stevens">John L. Stevens</a> and by troops from USS <em>Boston</em>, proclaimed a Provisional Government on 17 January, and the queen yielded ‘to the superior force of the United States of America’. The Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> followed in 1894; after a failed royalist rising the queen was forced to abdicate in 1895, and the United States annexed the islands in 1898.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> united the islands under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a> and became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> recognised by the great powers, with a literate public and modern institutions, but population collapse, the loss of land and dependence on American <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> markets left it open to the businessmen who, backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> troops, overthrew it in 1893.</em></p>

